        <div id="first-48-hours" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The First 48 Hours After a Leak or Flood
          </h2>

          <p>
            The EPA's mold guidance is blunt: dry water-damaged areas and items within 24 to 48 hours to prevent mold growth. That window is the reason restoration crews arrive with a truck full of equipment rather than one dehumidifier, and it is the reason a household unit set up in the corner of a soaked basement usually fails. A typical household dehumidifier is rated at 50 pints a day under the cold 2019 DOE test. A wet carpet pad and the bottom of a wall can hold many gallons.
          </p>

          <p>
            The order of work matters. First, stop the water and make the space safe: shut off the supply, and if water has reached outlets or the electrical panel, keep out until the power is off. Second, remove standing water by extraction, not evaporation. A wet vac or a rented extractor pulls out in an hour what a dehumidifier would take days to evaporate. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-vacuum-water-with-a-shop-vac/">vacuuming water with a shop vac</a> covers the technique.
          </p>

          <p>
            Third, open up what cannot dry in place. Wet carpet pad, saturated insulation and soaked cardboard boxes usually come out. Baseboards often come off, and restorers drill small holes or cut a flood line in drywall so air can reach the wall cavity. Only then do dehumidifiers and air movers go in, with the goal of evaporating the water left in materials and removing it from the air as fast as it evaporates.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fourth, keep the space closed. Opening windows feels intuitive but usually lets humid outdoor air in faster than it lets damp air out, especially in summer. Restoration drying is done as a closed system: air movers lift water out of materials, the dehumidifier pulls it out of the air, and the pump sends it down the drain. The rest of this guide is about choosing and sizing the dehumidifier side of that system.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Extract first, then dehumidify.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A wet vac or extractor removes liquid water in hours. A dehumidifier is for the water you cannot vacuum: the moisture inside carpet, pad, drywall and framing. Running a dehumidifier over standing water wastes days.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

