        <div id="lgr-explained" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What LGR Means, and Why Grain Depression Matters
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every unit in this guide is a low grain refrigerant, or LGR, dehumidifier. A conventional dehumidifier passes air over a cold coil and condenses water out of it, and it works well when the air is humid. As the room dries, it removes less and less, because the air reaching the coil is already close to the coil's dew point. LGR units pre-cool the incoming air with a heat exchanger before it reaches the main coil, so they keep removing water at humidity levels where a conventional unit stalls.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is exactly the situation in a drying job. On day one, the air is saturated and almost any dehumidifier pulls plenty of water. By day two or three, the air is drier but the materials still hold water, and the only way to keep drawing it out is to keep the air very dry. An LGR unit that can push the air down to a low moisture content keeps the evaporation going; a household unit that levels off at 45% to 50% relative humidity lets the drying stall.
          </p>

          <p>
            Restoration crews measure this as grain depression. Moisture in air is expressed in grains per pound (GPP), the weight of water vapor in a pound of dry air. Grain depression is the difference between the GPP of the air going into a dehumidifier and the air coming out. If the room air is at 80 GPP and the exhaust is at 45 GPP, the grain depression is 35. A bigger number means the unit is removing more water from each pass of air, and a falling number tells a technician the machine is struggling or the room is nearly dry.
          </p>

          <p>
            You do not need to calculate psychrometrics to dry a basement, but a thermo-hygrometer that shows temperature and relative humidity, or GPP directly, tells you whether the dehumidifier is working. Read it at the intake and the exhaust once or twice a day. Our <a href="/best-hygrometer/">hygrometer guide</a> covers affordable meters. If the numbers stop changing while the walls are still wet, you need more dehumidification, more airflow or a warmer room.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How Restoration Pros Size Dehumidifiers
          </h2>

          <p>
            Restoration companies size dehumidifiers using the IICRC S500 standard for water damage restoration. It sorts a loss two ways. The category describes how contaminated the water is: Category 1 is clean water from a supply line, Category 2 is grey water such as a washing machine overflow, and Category 3 is grossly contaminated water such as sewage or river flooding. The class describes how much water there is to evaporate, from Class 1, a small area with little absorption, to Class 3, where walls, ceilings and carpet are soaked, and Class 4, specialty drying of dense materials such as hardwood and concrete.
          </p>

          <p>
            For sizing, the IICRC publishes a chart of factors. You calculate the cubic feet of the affected space, divide by the factor for the class of water and type of dehumidifier, and get the total AHAM pints per day you need. For LGR units the factors are 100 for Class 1, 50 for Class 2 and 40 for Class 3; for conventional refrigerant units they are 100, 40 and 30. The IICRC describes the result as the minimum starting capacity, which a technician then adjusts by monitoring the job.
          </p>

          <p>
            Work an example. A 15 by 20 foot family room with an 8 foot ceiling is 2,400 cubic feet. If the carpet and pad are wet and water has wicked a little way up the walls, that is a typical Class 2 loss. Divide 2,400 by 50 and you need about 48 AHAM pints per day, comfortably one Storm LGR Extreme or Hurricane LGR85. A 30 by 40 foot finished basement at the same class is 9,600 cubic feet, which needs about 192 pints: two 85 pint units plus margin, one 125 pint unit and one compact, or a single Dri-Eaz LGR 3500i.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two lessons fall out of the arithmetic. First, a single household dehumidifier is undersized for anything beyond a small Class 1 leak, even before you account for its colder rating test. Second, several compact units often beat one big one, because they can be spread across rooms where the water is. If you are holding a large dry space at 50% afterwards rather than drying a loss, our <a href="/best-commercial-dehumidifier/">commercial dehumidifier guide</a> covers units sized for continuous duty.
          </p>

