        <div id="air-movers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Pairing Dehumidifiers With Air Movers
          </h2>

          <p>
            A dehumidifier removes water from the air; it does very little to get water out of a carpet or a wall. That job belongs to air movers, the low, snail-shell fans you see on restoration jobs, which blow a fast sheet of air across wet surfaces and speed up evaporation. Without them, a powerful LGR unit can dry the air in a room while the carpet pad underneath stays wet for days. With them, the dehumidifier has a steady supply of moisture to remove.
          </p>

          <p>
            Restoration training materials commonly cite IICRC guidance that starts with one air mover per affected room, then adds roughly one for every 50 to 70 square feet of wet floor, and more for wet walls and ceilings. That is a lot of fans: a soaked 300 square foot room might get four to six. Point them along the walls at a shallow angle so the air sweeps the base of the wall and the carpet, and keep them moving in the same direction around the room.
          </p>

          <p>
            The two halves have to be balanced. Air movers evaporate water into the air, and if the dehumidifier cannot keep up, the room turns into a steam bath and the moisture migrates into dry materials. If you can see condensation on windows or cold pipes, or your hygrometer shows the humidity climbing rather than falling, add dehumidification or remove a fan. The aim is a room that feels warm and dry while the materials slowly give up their water.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mind the power. Each air mover draws a couple of amps, a compact LGR unit draws about 5 to 6.5, and the Dri-Eaz LGR 3500i more than 10. Most homes have 15 amp circuits, and a basement may have only one or two. Spread the load across circuits, avoid daisy-chaining too many fans on one outlet, and use heavy extension cords. Warmth helps drying too: LGR units work best in the 70s to 80s °F, so do not turn the heat down while you dry.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="rent-or-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Renting vs Buying a Water Damage Dehumidifier
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most people searching for a dehumidifier for water damage are also searching for one to rent, and for a single event renting is often the right answer. Home Depot's tool rental lists Dri-Eaz units including the compact F413, which is the Revolution in this guide, and the larger F412. Independent rental yards and equipment suppliers carry similar Dri-Eaz, Phoenix and BlueDri gear, usually alongside air movers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rental rates vary widely by market, and Home Depot asks customers to check with their local store. Rental price guides we found put LGR dehumidifiers somewhere around $85 to $300 a day, often with air movers priced separately and sometimes with a minimum rental period. A typical dry-out takes three to five days, so a rented LGR unit and four air movers can easily run several hundred dollars, and more if the job stretches.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is where buying starts to make sense. An AlorAir Storm LGR Extreme at roughly $780 to $970, or an Abestorm Hurricane LGR85 at roughly $600 to $800, costs about the same as one longer rental, and afterwards it can serve as a heavy-duty basement or garage dehumidifier. If your home has a history of basement water, a sump that has failed before, or rental units you are responsible for, owning one LGR unit and a few air movers is a reasonable insurance policy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rent when the loss is large, when you need a big machine such as a Dri-Eaz LGR 3500i for only a few days, or when insurance will pay for professional drying anyway. Buy when you expect to use it again, when the rental counter is out of stock after a regional storm, which is exactly when everyone needs one, or when the unit will keep earning its place afterwards. Either way, keep the receipts for your insurer.
          </p>

