        <div id="wet-carpet" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Drying Wet Carpet With a Dehumidifier
          </h2>

          <p>
            Wet carpet is the most common reason people search for a dehumidifier after a leak, and the answer depends first on the water. If it was clean water from a supply line or a water heater and it was caught quickly, carpet can often be saved. If it was a washing machine, a dishwasher or anything that sat for days, the pad usually has to go. If it was sewage or outdoor floodwater, IICRC guidance treats porous materials such as carpet and pad as unsalvageable in most cases.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a clean-water loss, extract as much as possible first with a wet vac or a carpet extractor, making slow overlapping passes. Then restorers either dry the carpet in place with air movers blowing across it, or float it: pull the carpet back from the tack strip and run an air mover underneath so air flows between the carpet and the pad. The pad is the sponge in the system, and it is usually the last part to dry. A cheap pad is often replaced rather than dried.
          </p>

          <p>
            The dehumidifier's job is to keep the room air dry enough that the evaporating water has somewhere to go. Set it to its lowest humidity setting or continuous mode, close the room, keep the temperature in the 70s, and send the pump hose to a drain. A single compact LGR unit such as the Storm LGR Extreme or Hurricane LGR85 will usually handle one carpeted room; a whole carpeted basement calls for the sizing method above.
          </p>

          <p>
            A household carpet cleaner is not a substitute for an extractor on a soaked floor, but it is useful afterwards for cleaning and deodorizing carpet that was saved. Our <a href="/best-carpet-cleaner/">carpet cleaner guide</a> covers machines for that stage, and a <a href="/best-shop-vac/">good shop vac</a> is the first tool to reach for when water is still standing. If the carpet still smells musty after it feels dry, the pad or subfloor beneath is probably still wet.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="when-dry" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. How to Know When the Job Is Actually Dry
          </h2>

          <p>
            Dry to the touch is not dry. Drywall, framing and subfloor can feel dry on the surface while holding enough water inside to grow mold behind the paint. Restoration crews decide when to pull equipment by measuring, not by feel, and the same tools are cheap enough for a homeowner. The two that matter are a moisture meter for materials and a hygrometer for the air.
          </p>

          <p>
            The method is to compare wet materials with dry ones. Take a moisture meter reading on an unaffected wall in another room, then read the wet wall at several heights each day. When the wet wall reads about the same as the unaffected one, it has reached what restorers call the dry standard. Do the same for wood framing and subfloor. Keep a simple log with dates and readings; it is useful for an insurance claim and it stops you pulling the dehumidifier a day too early.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch the air too. A hygrometer in the room should show relative humidity falling over the first day or two and then holding low. If it climbs back up when you switch the dehumidifier off for an hour, the materials are still releasing water. Our <a href="/best-moisture-meter/">moisture meter guide</a> covers pin and pinless meters for drywall and wood, and the <a href="/best-hygrometer/">hygrometer guide</a> covers accurate room meters.
          </p>

          <p>
            Expect three to five days for a typical clean-water loss with adequate equipment, and longer for dense materials, cold rooms or a job that started late. Once materials are back to their dry standard, a compact LGR unit can stay on as an ordinary basement dehumidifier, set to about 50%, for the rest of the season. If a basement floods regularly, see our guide to <a href="/water-coming-through-basement-floor/">water coming through a basement floor</a> for the underlying fix.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="call-a-pro" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. When to Call a Restoration Professional
          </h2>

          <p>
            A homeowner with the right equipment can dry a clean-water leak in a room or two. Some losses are a different job. Category 3 water, such as sewage backups and outdoor floodwater, carries bacteria and contaminants that call for protective equipment, removal of porous materials and cleaning that goes well beyond drying. Water that has been sitting for more than a couple of days, or that has soaked into ceilings, insulation or hardwood floors, is also a candidate for professional drying.
          </p>

          <p>
            Insurance is the other reason. Many homeowner policies cover sudden water damage such as a burst pipe, and insurers often expect drying to be documented with moisture readings and equipment logs. A restoration company does that as a matter of course and bills the insurer directly. Call your insurer before you start tearing out materials, take photos of everything, and keep the damaged items until an adjuster has seen them, unless they pose a health risk.
          </p>

          <p>
            If mold has already appeared, drying is only half the job. Visible growth larger than about 10 square feet, the size EPA guidance uses for when to consider professional help, or any growth inside HVAC ducts, is beyond most DIY cleanup. Our guides to <a href="/mold-remediation-cost/">mold remediation cost</a> and <a href="/how-to-get-rid-of-mold-in-basement/">getting rid of mold in a basement</a> explain what to expect.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, think about prevention once the space is dry. A water alarm next to the water heater and washing machine, a working sump pump with a battery backup, and a dehumidifier holding the basement at about 50% prevent many of the losses this guide is about. Our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-home/">best dehumidifiers for the home</a> guide covers everyday units, and the <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">basement guide</a> covers larger ones for keeping a lower level dry year-round.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a household dehumidifier for a flood</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 50 pint household unit rated under the cold 2019 DOE test cannot keep pace with the water evaporating from soaked carpet and drywall, and it levels off as the air dries. The result is a slow dry-out that misses the 24 to 48 hour window for preventing mold. Use LGR equipment sized to the room.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running a dehumidifier over standing water</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Evaporating liquid water takes days and overwhelms any dehumidifier. Extract standing water with a wet vac or extractor first, pull up soaked pad and insulation, and only then set up dehumidifiers and air movers to dry what remains in materials. Extraction removes far more water per hour than evaporation.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Opening windows to let the house breathe</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Restoration drying works as a closed system. In humid weather, open windows let moist outdoor air in faster than damp air leaves, so the dehumidifier ends up drying the neighborhood. Close the space, keep it warm, and let the air movers and dehumidifier do the work together.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the air movers</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A dehumidifier dries air, not carpet pad or the inside of a wall. Without air movers sweeping wet surfaces, a powerful LGR unit can dry the room while the materials stay wet for days. Plan on one air mover per room plus more for large wet floor areas and walls.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pulling equipment when surfaces feel dry</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Drywall and framing can feel dry while holding enough water inside to grow mold. Measure with a moisture meter and compare with unaffected materials elsewhere in the house. Keep the dehumidifier running until wet materials match that dry standard, then keep a log for your insurer.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What kind of dehumidifier is best for water damage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A low grain refrigerant (LGR) dehumidifier with a built-in pump. LGR units keep removing water as the air dries, which a household unit does not, and the pump lets them run unattended. Compact models such as the AlorAir Storm LGR Extreme and Abestorm Hurricane LGR85 suit a room or two; larger losses need the Dri-Eaz LGR 3500i or several units.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should I run a dehumidifier after water damage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Until the wet materials are as dry as unaffected materials elsewhere in the house, measured with a moisture meter. For a typical clean-water loss with enough dehumidifiers and air movers, that usually takes three to five days. Dense materials, cool rooms and jobs that started late take longer. Do not stop when the surface feels dry.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a dehumidifier dry wet carpet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It helps, but it cannot do it alone. Extract water first with a wet vac or carpet extractor, then use air movers across or under the carpet to evaporate what remains, while the dehumidifier removes that moisture from the air. Carpet pad is often replaced rather than dried, and carpet soaked by sewage or floodwater is usually discarded.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many dehumidifiers do I need for water damage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Restorers use the IICRC method: calculate the room's cubic feet and divide by a factor for the class of water. For LGR units the factor is 50 for a typical Class 2 loss. A 2,400 cubic foot room needs about 48 AHAM pints, one compact unit. A 9,600 cubic foot basement needs about 192 pints, two or three units.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it cheaper to rent or buy a water damage dehumidifier?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a single small leak, renting is often cheaper. Rental guides put LGR units around $85 to $300 a day, and a dry-out takes three to five days, often with air movers on top. A budget LGR unit costs roughly $600 to $1,000, so buying makes sense if you expect to use it again or want it as a basement dehumidifier afterwards.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What does grain depression mean?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Grain depression is the difference between the moisture content of air entering a dehumidifier and air leaving it, measured in grains of water per pound of dry air. A higher figure means the unit is removing more water from each pass. Restoration technicians track it to confirm a dehumidifier is working and to decide when a room is nearly dry.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How soon after a leak will mold grow?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                EPA guidance says to dry water-damaged areas and items within 24 to 48 hours to prevent mold growth. That is why fast extraction and properly sized drying equipment matter more than the brand of dehumidifier. If materials have been wet longer, or mold is already visible over a large area, consider professional restoration and remediation.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

